---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import DosageCalculator from '../components/dosage/DosageCalculator';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-dosage-calculator`;

const medicalPageJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D Dosage Calculator',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  description:
    'Calculate the loading dose of vitamin D3 needed to raise your serum 25(OH)D to a target level, plus the daily maintenance dose to hold it there. Weight-based, BMI-adjusted, using the Van Groningen formula.',
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: {
    '@type': 'MedicalCondition',
    name: 'Vitamin D deficiency',
    code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'E55', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' },
  },
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};

const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D Dosage Calculator', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};

const softwareJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
  name: 'Vitamin D Dosage Calculator',
  applicationCategory: 'HealthApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Web',
  offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  url: PAGE_URL,
  description:
    'Weight-based vitamin D3 loading and maintenance dose calculator using the Van Groningen (2010) repletion formula.',
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How much vitamin D do I need to raise my level?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'A validated estimate is 40 IU of vitamin D3 per kilogram of body weight for every 1 nmol/L (0.4 ng/mL) you want to raise serum 25(OH)D. For example, an 80 kg adult raising from 15 to 30 ng/mL (a 37.5 nmol/L gap) needs roughly 120,000 IU total, spread over several weeks.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What is a safe daily maintenance dose of vitamin D?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'For most adults, 1,000–2,000 IU/day maintains a sufficient level, with 3,000–4,000 IU/day for people who are obese or have malabsorption. 4,000 IU/day is the tolerable upper intake level set by the Institute of Medicine for adults.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D Dosage Calculator — Loading & Maintenance Dose by Weight"
  description="Free vitamin D dosage calculator. Enter your current and target 25(OH)D level and body weight to get the loading dose and daily maintenance dose of vitamin D3 you need. Based on the validated Van Groningen formula."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalPageJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(softwareJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-dosage-calculator", label: "Dosage Calculator" }]} />

  <DosageCalculator client:load />

  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16">
    <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">How the dose is calculated</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      There are two separate questions in vitamin D dosing, and mixing them up is the most common
      mistake. The first is <strong>how do I get from where I am to where I want to be</strong> — the
      loading (repletion) dose. The second is <strong>how do I stay there</strong> — the daily
      maintenance dose. This calculator answers both.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">
      The loading dose uses the weight-based formula validated by Van Groningen and colleagues (2010)
      in 211 deficient outpatients:
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-800 leading-relaxed mt-3 bg-gray-100 rounded-lg px-4 py-3 font-mono text-sm">
      total dose (IU) = 40 &times; (target &minus; current, in nmol/L) &times; body weight (kg)
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">
      In plain terms, roughly <strong>40 IU of cholecalciferol per kilogram of body weight raises
      serum 25(OH)D by about 1 nmol/L</strong> (0.4 ng/mL). Because body weight is built into the
      equation, it already absorbs most of the reason heavier people need more — vitamin D is
      fat-soluble and distributes into a larger volume (Drincic 2012). That total is then delivered
      over a few weeks, either as the classic 50,000 IU weekly regimen or as a daily equivalent.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Choosing a target level</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Guidelines disagree on the ideal 25(OH)D level. The Institute of Medicine considers
      20 ng/mL (50 nmol/L) adequate for bone health in most people, while the Endocrine Society
      suggests 30 ng/mL (75 nmol/L). There is no trial evidence that pushing levels above roughly
      50 ng/mL improves outcomes, and levels sustained above ~100–150 ng/mL risk hypercalcaemia.
      A sensible target for most adults is <strong>30–50 ng/mL</strong>; this tool warns you if you
      set a target outside the evidence-supported band. See our{' '}
      <a href="/vitamin-d-testing-guide" class="text-amber-700 underline">testing guide</a> and{' '}
      <a href="/vitamin-d-dosage" class="text-amber-700 underline">dosage guide</a> for the full picture.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Maintenance dosing</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Once you have repleted, a steady daily dose holds the level. Dose-response data (Heaney 2003)
      show roughly 1 ng/mL of steady-state rise per 100 IU/day in lean adults. People with obesity
      need about 1.5&ndash;2&times; more, and those with fat malabsorption (coeliac disease, Crohn's,
      bariatric surgery, cystic fibrosis, cholestatic liver disease) can need 2&ndash;3&times; more or
      a specialised formulation. The calculator scales the maintenance figure accordingly and caps it
      at 10,000 IU/day, above which supervision and monitoring are essential.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">When not to self-dose</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Some conditions turn vitamin D from benign into risky. In <a href="/vitamin-d-and-sarcoidosis" class="text-amber-700 underline">sarcoidosis</a> and
      other granulomatous diseases, unregulated conversion to active vitamin D can cause dangerous
      hypercalcaemia at ordinary doses. In primary hyperparathyroidism, advanced kidney disease, and
      while taking thiazide diuretics, dosing must be individualised by a clinician. This calculator
      is an educational estimate, not a substitute for that judgement.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Key citations</h2>
    <ul class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
      <li>van Groningen L et al. Cholecalciferol loading dose guideline for vitamin D-deficient adults. <em>Eur J Endocrinol</em>. 2010;162(4):805&ndash;811.</li>
      <li>Heaney RP et al. Human serum 25-hydroxycholecalciferol response to extended oral dosing with cholecalciferol. <em>Am J Clin Nutr</em>. 2003;77(1):204&ndash;210.</li>
      <li>Holick MF et al. Evaluation, treatment, and prevention of vitamin D deficiency: an Endocrine Society clinical practice guideline. <em>J Clin Endocrinol Metab</em>. 2011;96(7):1911&ndash;1930.</li>
      <li>Institute of Medicine. Dietary Reference Intakes for Calcium and Vitamin D. National Academies Press; 2011.</li>
      <li>Drincic AT et al. Volumetric dilution, rather than sequestration, best explains the low vitamin D status of obesity. <em>Obesity</em>. 2012;20(7):1444&ndash;1448.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-dosage-calculator" />
</BaseLayout>
